<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Shah of Malacca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1528">1528</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> was the last sultan of Malacca, ruling from 1488 until the Portuguese under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> captured the city in 1511. He fled and kept up resistance from Pahang, Bintan and finally Kampar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, where he died in 1528. His sons founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and Perak sultanates, continuing the Malacca dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-shah-of-malacca.jpg" alt="Mahmud Shah of Malacca" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_Shah_of_Malacca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahmud Shah was the son of Sultan Alauddin Riayat Shah and was installed at a very young age after his father's early death. The government lay in the hands of the bendahara, or prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>, who acted as regent. As a young man the sultan was known as a ruthless ruler. He is associated with the Malay legend of Puteri Gunung Ledang, the story of his failed courtship of a celestial princess, and with warriors of his court such as Hang Tuah and Hang Nadim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tun-perak">Tun Perak</a>'s death in 1498 is said to have made Mahmud a more responsible ruler, but his later years were turbulent. The new bendahara, Tun Mutahir, took bribes and appointed ministers on a whim, and factions formed at court. Deceived by one faction, which accused Tun Mutahir of planning a rebellion, the sultan had him and his whole family executed; Tun Mutahir had also refused to let him marry his daughter, Tun Fatimah. The defences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> weakened, its army being largely made up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who were disloyal to the sultan.</p><p>During the visit of the Portuguese admiral Diogo Lopes de Sequeira in 1509–1510 the sultan planned to have him killed, but Sequeira learned of the plot and escaped. The episode gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-de-albuquerque">Afonso de Albuquerque</a> a reason for the attack that ended in the Capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> in 1511.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mahmud fled to Pahang, where he sought Chinese aid in vain, then moved south to Bintan, where he still received tribute from states that had owed allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>. He led a Malay Muslim confederacy that attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a> several times between 1515 and 1519 without success. His son Ahmad Shah, who had succeeded him, was judged incompetent and was killed by Mahmud himself in 1513 after a failed attempt to retake the city, and Mahmud took the title again as a pretender. In 1526 the Portuguese destroyed his capital on Bintan, and he fled to Kampar, where he died two years later.</p><p>His son Raja Ali, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-riayat-shah-ii">Alauddin Riayat Shah II</a>, founded the Johor Sultanate, and another son, Muzaffar, was invited by the Perak ministers Tun Saban and Nakhoda Kassim to found the Perak Sultanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Shah of Malacca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Malacca fell to Portugal in 1511, <b>Sultan Mahmud Shah</b>'s eldest son, Raja Muzaffar, was invited by the people of the Perak River valley to be their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/perak-sultanate">Perak Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Kampar, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=0.31,101.02&amp;year=1518" aria-label="Kampar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7356 2152 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mahmud Shah of Malacca"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7806 2422h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7806 2422h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=0.31,101.02&amp;year=1518">What was here in 1518? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
